<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    THE UNSHIPPED CAPTURE HOST.

    It drives the loop docs/04_CAPTURE.md specifies and nothing else does yet:
    HookedCaptureGate to FlGuardedInject to ShmRingReader.TryAttach to a ~10 Hz
    drain, with GuardSupervisor.ScanOnceAsync and PublishGuardResult beside it.
    It is the FIRST PRODUCTION DRIVER of FlControlBlock.guardTicks, the sending
    half of the 30 s re-scan that docs/19_SAFETY_AND_ANTICHEAT.md calls the most
    important runtime behaviour in the capture layer. Both ends of that field
    existed and were tested; only the loop was missing.

    WHY IT IS A PROJECT OF ITS OWN AND NOT A FLAG ON FrameLedger.Agent.

    docs/12_BUILD.md publishes exactly two roots, FrameLedger.App and
    FrameLedger.Agent, into one out/app. A project that neither references is
    outside the package BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a flag someone could
    flip. 20_OPEN_QUESTIONS §S27 is why that matters: a capture flag on a shipped,
    self-contained binary would have loaded the Overlay into any x64 process a
    user named, with no game record, no consent stamp and the 19_SAFETY
    disclosure never shown. Every anti-cheat check would still have run, so it
    was not an anti-cheat bypass; it was a consent and disclosure gap in a
    shipped binary, which is its own class.

    tools/package-closure-check.ps1 is what keeps that true. Referencing this
    project from either publish root turns the gate red and names the edge.

    EVERY TYPE HERE IS internal. .editorconfig relaxes CA1515 only under
    src/FrameLedger.App, and FrameLedger.Agent's own Program is internal for the
    same reason. InternalsVisibleTo below is what lets the test project see them.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <AssemblyName>FrameLedger.CaptureHost</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>FrameLedger.CaptureHost</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      Directory.Build.props deliberately does NOT set RuntimeIdentifier: forcing a
      RID onto class libraries pulls RID-specific assets into their restore for no
      benefit, so the two executables set it themselves. docs/12_BUILD.md claimed
      the props file sets it, which is corrected in this PR.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="FrameLedger.CaptureHost.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FrameLedger.Application\FrameLedger.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FrameLedger.Infrastructure\FrameLedger.Infrastructure.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FrameLedger.Shared\FrameLedger.Shared.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    All three native staging targets, and each is load-bearing rather than
    defensive:

      Guard   - NativeAntiCheatGuard loads FrameLedger.Guard.dll BY ABSOLUTE PATH
                from AppContext.BaseDirectory and never falls back to a search, so
                the file has to actually be there.
      Overlay - §S22 makes the guard refuse a payload that does not resolve into
                the directory the guard's own code was loaded from, compared by
                file id. Without this every injection answers PayloadNotOurs.
      Rules   - without a seeded blocklist the guard answers RulesUnreadable for
                EVERY target (§S20). That was the first real injection's opening
                refusal, and CI hit it again in the drain test.

    FrameLedger.Guard.targets records "imported by exactly two projects" and
    §S18 blocker 3's ratification that the Agent is the sole host of the guard.
    This makes it three; that comment and the re-ratification are updated in the
    same PR. What §S18 blocker 3 was FOR - the WPF UI acquiring the guard as a
    side effect of wanting SQLite - does not apply to a deliberate host, and the
    fix it produced is a property of the DIRECTORY rather than of the process.

    NOT FrameLedger.DrainFixtures.targets: hook-harness is a test fixture, and
    staging it beside a host binary is how a host acquires a target of its own.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\FrameLedger.Guard.targets" />
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\FrameLedger.Rules.targets" />
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\FrameLedger.Overlay.targets" />

  <!--
    fl-probe-nvapi.exe beside the host, the way the Overlay is: NgxDriverProbe spawns it
    by absolute path from AppContext.BaseDirectory and reports ProbeMissing rather than
    searching. Warn-not-fail for the same reason the Overlay targets do: build.ps1 runs
    the native build first. UNSHIPPED with this host; the Agent would call NVAPI through
    Infrastructure, not a child process.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <FlNativeProbeDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\build\native\x64-release\tools\fl-probe-nvapi\</FlNativeProbeDir>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(FlNativeProbeDir)fl-probe-nvapi.exe" Condition="Exists('$(FlNativeProbeDir)fl-probe-nvapi.exe')">
      <Link>fl-probe-nvapi.exe</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
